      'Municipal securities are exempt from federal regulations. However, a self-regulatory body, the Municipal Securities Rulemaking Board (MSRB), regulates broker-dealers and advisers who deal in municipal securities, but it has no authority over municipal issuers, which are self-governing. Because municipal securities are exempt from registration, issuers do not have to publish a prospectus, which is a detailed disclosure document required of most issuers of securities.\n\nInstead, they offer a less detailed disclosure document called an official statement. This document contains information about the bonds and the financial condition of the issuer. Firms that sell municipal securities are required to give their customers copies of the official statement by the confirmation of the trade.\n\nAll municipal issues have a legal opinion. The legal opinion tells the investor that the municipality is authorized to sell the bonds. To get this opinion, the municipality issuing the securities will retain a law firm to act as bond counsel and examine the issue to make sure of its:\n\n• Legality: It is legally binding on the issuer. e Validity: Fully authorized and issued by municipality. • Tax-exempt status: The interest is exempt from federal tax under current law.\n\nThe bond counsel prepares all the legal documentation necessary for the issue including the legal opinion. There are two types of legal opinions that can be given: Qualified: The opinion will be qualified if the counsel finds a problem. There are two types of legal opinions that can be given:\n\nQualified: The opinion will be qualified if the counsel finds a problem. The qualifier will state that there is a legal uncertainty of which any purchaser should be aware. For example, new federal tax rulings may make the interest taxable.\n\n•Unqualified: The bond counsel determines that they have found no problems with the issue.\n\nIn addition to a legal opinion, all municipal bonds are issued under a bond contract prepared by the bond counsel. The contract consists of various pieces, depending on the type of issue. Every bond contract has:\n\n• An authorizing resolution, known as a bond resolution, which allows the sale of the securities\n• Asecurity agreement pledging an income source to back the issue, such as full faith, credit, and taxing power\n\nfor a general obligation(GO) bond or specified revenues for a revenue bond Municipal debt maturities can be either long-term debt or short-term notes. for a general obligation(GO) bond or specified revenues for a revenue bond\n\nMunicipal debt maturities can be either long-term debt or short-term notes. Most long-term issues are serial bonds with maturities spread over a sequence of years. These bonds are issued at par and pay interest semiannually.\n\nShort-term notes can be issued in two ways:\n\n• Ata discount to par, which matures at par value • Atpar, which matures at par plus accrued interest\n\nCharacteristics of Municipal Bonds\n\n• Interest income not taxed at the federal level • Exempt from registration, but not from anti-fraud requirements • Come with an official statement instead of a prospectus • Good for investors in higher tax brackets • Typically, lower yields than corporate bonds but higher than Treasuries e Typically, less risky than corporate bonds • Good for portfolio diversification\n\nMunicipal issues are broadly categorized into general obligation bonds, revenue bonds, special types of bonds, and short-term notes.',
