      'A general obligation bond (GO bond) is backed by the full faith, credit, and taxing power of the issuer. GO bonds are typically backed by tax revenue. The type of tax that backs GO bonds depends on the issuing entity.\n\nLocal governments can collect property taxes, known as ad valorem taxes. Local governmental issues are usually backed by unlimited ad valorem taxes. This means that the municipality promises to raise taxes, without any limit, to pay off the bondholders.\n\nIn some cases, issuers will sell limited tax bonds. This means that there is a limit placed on the rate that the issuer is allowed to assess. The bondholders would be at risk if the issuer is not collecting enough taxes to service the debt.\n\nAs a result, limited tax bonds have more credit risk than unlimited tax bonds and sell at higher interest rates.\n\nMany municipalities also have imposed a statutory debt limit on the dollar amount of GO bonds that can be outstanding at any one time. In this case, a municipality cannot issue additional GO bonds if it is already at its debt limit. The only way to change the maximum debt limit is through legislative action or a public referendum.\n\nGO bondholders have the right to compel a tax levy or legislative appropriation to make payment on the debt in the event of a default. Because of this, GO bonds are generally considered the safest type of municipal bonds. The rating of each issuer can vary and is provided by the ratings services.\n\nCapital appreciation bonds (CABs) are municipal zero-coupon bonds with a legal twist. CABs are usually sold at a deep discount to par, and only the discounted principal amount is counted against the issuer\'s debt limit. For this reason, CABs are good for municipalities that are close to their debt limit.',
